@vsm 1.0
// EXP-VNC-01/spec.vsm — V negative control: does the viability functional DISCRIMINATE?
// domain_context: {target=vsf-nucleus, env=analysis, layer=L+2}
// date: 2026-06-15 | status: PRE-REGISTERED (hypotheses fixed before running)
// suite: certifier-validation (with EXP-G-01, EXP-CORE-01) | binary: v_negative_control (main.rs, TBD)

spec(EXP-VNC-01, "negative control for the viability functional V = min(g,c,t,m)"):

  motivation:
    "V is currently measured ONLY on the healthy kernel (signature [g≈1,p≈1,c≈1,t=1,m(σ)],
     V(σ)=0.46..0.99). A certificate that is only ever evaluated on viable systems and always
     returns a 'reasonable' number is NOT shown to be a certificate. EXP-VNC-01 is the
     falsification test of the §capstone: a genuinely non-viable configuration MUST give V→0.
     Without a measured low-V example, the V claim is unfalsified."

  design:
    "Compute the FULL V signature [g,p,c,t,m] for a panel of configurations — healthy kernel,
     the existing architecture ablations (NC-01 no S5, NC-02 constant trust, NC-03 no S2), and
     constructed per-axis breakers — and show that V discriminates (some give V≈0). Because V is
     a strict min, breaking ANY single axis must collapse V; we demonstrate each axis can do so."

  breakers (one per V axis — each must independently drive V→0):
    g_break: "violate a constraint of Ω (budget B(task)>B_available, or governance autonomy>ceiling,
              or an injected A0 face: purpose∩prohibited≠∅). Expect slack<0 ⇒ g=0 ⇒ V=0 even with
              c,t,m≈1. (Shares mechanism with EXP-G-01; here scored at the V level.)"
    c_break: "make the map non-contractive: relaxation coefficient a≥1 (e.g. a=1.01, expansive) so
              λ_max=ln(a)>0. Expect the sign-gate c=½(1+tanh(−λ_max/σ̂_λ))→0 ⇒ V=0."
    t_break: "drive a periodic forcing that produces a limit cycle (β₁=1). Expect t→0 ⇒ V=0.
              Contrast with the kernel's measured β₀=1∧β₁=0 (point sink)."
    m_break: "the kernel itself has NO finite noise ceiling (EXP-NS-01, m saturates ≥0.33), so m
              cannot be driven to 0 on the healthy kernel — this is a FEATURE. The m-breaker is a
              shallow-basin variant (weak contraction a→1⁻) whose steady-state excursion reaches
              ε_frontier ⇒ m→0. Demonstrates m is not vacuously high."

  hypotheses (pre-registered):
    H_VNC1: "∃ a constructible configuration with V≈0 — V discriminates viable from non-viable."
    H_VNC2: "each of g, c, t (and m via the shallow-basin variant) can INDEPENDENTLY drive V→0;
             the min operator means any single axis failure collapses the certificate."
    H_VNC3: "PRE-REGISTERED OPEN — do the architecture ablations NC-01/02/03 give LOW V, or do
             they (as Exp 11 found) stay viable? If V stays high under ablation, that SHARPENS the
             claim: V scores the viability of the STATE; architecture adds observability/intention,
             not raw survival. Honest either way — ties V back to §negative-controls."

  deliverable:
    "A table of V signatures across {healthy, NC-01, NC-02, NC-03, g/c/t/m-breakers} showing V≈0
     for the genuine breakers. The first measured demonstration that V is a discriminator, not a
     number that happens to be high. Closes the falsification gap of the §capstone."

  paper_impact:
    "§capstone — new 'V discriminates' paragraph + table; the strongest single answer to the
     reviewer objection 'you only ever measured V on a viable system'. Links to §negative-controls."

  implementation_notes:
    "Reuse basin_mc/sustained_noise machinery for the signature computation. c_break needs a
     configurable relaxation coefficient (env VSF_RELAX_A). m_break = same with a→1⁻. t_break needs
     a periodic kick injector + the existing vr_complex β computation. g_break overlaps EXP-G-01.
     Analysis-only; the operational kernel path is untouched."

⟦ /EXP-VNC-01-spec | vsm-1.0 | 2026-06-15 | PRE-REGISTERED ⟧
